    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper provides an in-depth exploration of the Black Panther Party's (BPP) pioneering educational programs, from their inception with Liberation Schools to the establishment of the Oakland Community School (OCS). Through historical analysis, the paper highlights how the  Black Panther Party leveraged education as a tool for empowerment, fostering critical thinking, cultural identity, and self-knowledge within Black communities. Faced with public schools that often underserved Black youth, the  Black Panther Party crafted alternative learning environments that championed student-centered, culturally relevant curricula, emphasizing a liberatory pedagogy and holistic support. These educational initiatives were grounded in the Black Panther Party's commitment to community-building, self-reliance, and systemic reform, challenging prevailing inequities in the U.S. educational system. By dissecting the philosophies, challenges, and achievements of the Black Panther Party’s schools, the paper underscores the lasting impact of the Black Panther Party's educational model, which continues to influence contemporary movements for educational justice and equity.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
